1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:08,481
On April twenty-third, 1985, Coca-Cola made
an announcement that would become one of

2
00:00:08,521 --> 00:00:11,691
the most infamous decisions
in business history.

3
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:15,466
The company was replacing the
taste of its flagship drink.

4
00:00:15,959 --> 00:00:20,462
It had tested the new formula on
nearly two hundred thousand consumers.

5
00:00:21,164 --> 00:00:22,881
The research looked convincing.

6
00:00:23,505 --> 00:00:28,695
But Americans responded with anger,
protests, and demands for the original Coke.

7
00:00:29,028 --> 00:00:32,177
Just seventy-nine days later,
the company reversed course.

8
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:37,686
How did a brand with extraordinary consumer
research misunderstand its own customers

9
00:00:37,726 --> 00:00:38,816
so badly?

10
00:00:40,518 --> 00:00:43,626
Coca-Cola began in Atlanta in 1886.

11
00:00:44,551 --> 00:00:48,711
Over the next century, it became
much more than a soft drink.

12
00:00:48,751 --> 00:00:53,228
Its distinctive bottle, red-and-white
identity, advertising, and place in American

13
00:00:53,268 --> 00:00:56,678
culture created an
unusually powerful brand.

14
00:00:57,182 --> 00:01:01,401
Generations associated Coca-Cola with
familiar experiences, family gatherings,

15
00:01:01,441 --> 00:01:04,531
sporting events, and everyday routines.

16
00:01:05,276 --> 00:01:10,410
The company sold refreshment, but it
also sold recognition and continuity.

17
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:14,642
By the early 1980s, that history
had become a commercial advantage.

18
00:01:15,266 --> 00:01:20,060
It also created an obligation the company's
executives would soon underestimate.

19
00:01:22,217 --> 00:01:23,626
The pressure came from Pepsi.

20
00:01:24,261 --> 00:01:30,210
Throughout the 1970s and early 1980s, Pepsi
challenged Coca-Cola's position in the

21
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:31,800
American cola market.

22
00:01:32,259 --> 00:01:37,051
Its advertising appealed to younger consumers,
while the Pepsi Challenge used blind

23
00:01:37,091 --> 00:01:39,621
taste tests to question
Coke's superiority.

24
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:44,810
Coca-Cola remained enormously successful,
but management worried about a

25
00:01:44,850 --> 00:01:47,860
long-running decline in
its lead over its rival.

26
00:01:48,371 --> 00:01:50,329
The competitive threat was real.

27
00:01:50,859 --> 00:01:53,029
The question was how to respond.

28
00:01:53,739 --> 00:01:57,992
Should Coca-Cola defend its distinctive
identity, or change its core product to

29
00:01:58,032 --> 00:02:00,062
compete more directly on taste?

30
00:02:01,976 --> 00:02:06,396
Coca-Cola researchers developed a sweeter
formula that performed strongly in taste

31
00:02:06,436 --> 00:02:07,436
tests.

32
00:02:07,846 --> 00:02:12,823
Across several years, the company tested
nearly two hundred thousand consumers in

33
00:02:12,863 --> 00:02:14,733
the United States and Canada.

34
00:02:15,242 --> 00:02:19,922
Contemporary reporting described a fifty-five
to forty-five preference for the new

35
00:02:19,962 --> 00:02:22,752
formula over the old one
in blind comparisons.

36
00:02:23,491 --> 00:02:27,123
To executives, the result
suggested a major opportunity.

37
00:02:27,746 --> 00:02:32,046
If customers preferred the new taste,
perhaps changing the flagship drink could

38
00:02:32,086 --> 00:02:33,156
restore momentum.

39
00:02:34,116 --> 00:02:38,601
But a laboratory preference is not the same
thing as a purchasing decision made in a

40
00:02:38,641 --> 00:02:39,641
store.

41
00:02:40,892 --> 00:02:44,040
The weakness was not simply
that Coca-Cola lacked data.

42
00:02:44,551 --> 00:02:47,497
It had enormous amounts
of data about taste.

43
00:02:48,155 --> 00:02:51,532
The problem was what that
data could not fully explain.

44
00:02:52,176 --> 00:02:56,746
People evaluating anonymous samples were
making a different decision from loyal

45
00:02:56,786 --> 00:03:00,776
customers being told that their
familiar drink would disappear.

46
00:03:01,328 --> 00:03:04,490
One question was which
liquid tasted better.

47
00:03:04,927 --> 00:03:09,376
Another was whether consumers wanted the
company to replace something they loved.

48
00:03:09,904 --> 00:03:12,275
Those questions were not interchangeable.

49
00:03:12,867 --> 00:03:17,282
Coca-Cola's research emphasized the first,
while its most passionate customers cared

50
00:03:17,322 --> 00:03:19,552
deeply about the second.

51
00:03:21,270 --> 00:03:23,738
The decision reached the top of Coca-Cola.

52
00:03:24,202 --> 00:03:29,647
Chairman and chief executive Roberto
Goizueta championed the change, supported by

53
00:03:29,687 --> 00:03:30,687
senior leadership.

54
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:35,550
The company believed it could strengthen
its position against Pepsi by introducing a

55
00:03:35,590 --> 00:03:37,660
product that tested better with consumers.

56
00:03:38,393 --> 00:03:40,988
But this was not simply
another flavor extension.

57
00:03:41,608 --> 00:03:47,179
Management chose to replace the original
taste of its flagship American cola.

58
00:03:47,514 --> 00:03:51,976
That distinction transformed a product-
development decision into a challenge to the

59
00:03:52,016 --> 00:03:53,166
brand's identity.

60
00:03:53,693 --> 00:03:58,310
Coca-Cola was not asking customers to try
something new alongside the familiar.

61
00:03:58,953 --> 00:04:00,906
It was taking the familiar away.

62
00:04:02,608 --> 00:04:07,821
At New York's Lincoln Center, Coca-Cola
publicly unveiled its reformulated drink.

63
00:04:08,319 --> 00:04:13,380
The new version was promoted as a major
improvement, backed by years of research and

64
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:14,570
consumer testing.

65
00:04:15,409 --> 00:04:17,808
Packaging was updated
to signal the change.

66
00:04:18,414 --> 00:04:23,216
The announcement received enormous attention,
exactly what a company might hope for

67
00:04:23,256 --> 00:04:25,086
during a major product launch.

68
00:04:25,653 --> 00:04:28,364
But the attention quickly
became difficult to control.

69
00:04:28,967 --> 00:04:33,628
For many customers, the news wasn't that
Coca-Cola had created an exciting new

70
00:04:33,668 --> 00:04:34,668
drink.

71
00:04:35,035 --> 00:04:39,836
It was that the company had decided their
old Coca-Cola was no longer available.

72
00:04:41,710 --> 00:04:44,059
The reaction was immediate and emotional.

73
00:04:44,725 --> 00:04:47,300
Customers contacted Coca-Cola to complain.

74
00:04:47,891 --> 00:04:51,834
Some began stockpiling the original
drink before supplies disappeared.

75
00:04:52,521 --> 00:04:56,233
Protest groups formed, demanding
the return of the old formula.

76
00:04:56,770 --> 00:05:01,291
In Atlanta, the company's own hometown,
public criticism became particularly

77
00:05:01,331 --> 00:05:02,331
visible.

78
00:05:02,731 --> 00:05:05,378
The scale of the response
surprised management.

79
00:05:05,885 --> 00:05:10,667
What executives had treated as a taste
improvement was being interpreted by some

80
00:05:10,707 --> 00:05:14,717
consumers as the removal of
something culturally important.

81
00:05:15,384 --> 00:05:19,662
The company had underestimated how strongly
people could identify with an everyday

82
00:05:19,702 --> 00:05:20,702
product.

83
00:05:22,271 --> 00:05:27,649
By June, Coca-Cola reported receiving
approximately fifteen hundred calls a day on

84
00:05:27,689 --> 00:05:32,579
its consumer hotline, compared with around
four hundred before the formula change.

85
00:05:33,178 --> 00:05:37,615
Employees across the company were confronted
by people demanding an explanation.

86
00:05:38,443 --> 00:05:40,999
The protests became a national news story.

87
00:05:41,426 --> 00:05:45,736
This was no longer a routine product
launch receiving mixed reviews.

88
00:05:46,398 --> 00:05:51,142
It was a public challenge to the
company's understanding of its own brand.

89
00:05:51,275 --> 00:05:55,880
The backlash also revealed something
that sales forecasts and taste tests had

90
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:57,190
struggled to quantify.

91
00:05:57,686 --> 00:06:01,219
Customers felt a sense of ownership
over Coca-Cola's identity.

92
00:06:03,388 --> 00:06:06,924
Pepsi watched its rival face
a public-relations crisis.

93
00:06:07,634 --> 00:06:11,941
The reformulation appeared to validate
Pepsi's long-running argument that taste

94
00:06:11,981 --> 00:06:13,711
preferences were shifting.

95
00:06:14,267 --> 00:06:19,043
Contemporary coverage captured the competitive
theater surrounding the launch, with

96
00:06:19,083 --> 00:06:22,513
Pepsi executives celebrating
Coca-Cola's decision.

97
00:06:23,105 --> 00:06:26,297
But Pepsi had not forced
consumers to reject New Coke.

98
00:06:26,781 --> 00:06:29,991
The backlash came from
Coca-Cola's own loyal audience.

99
00:06:30,641 --> 00:06:33,408
That made the situation
particularly damaging.

100
00:06:34,014 --> 00:06:38,757
The company had changed its product partly
because it feared losing customers to a

101
00:06:38,797 --> 00:06:39,797
rival.

102
00:06:39,901 --> 00:06:44,589
Instead, it had angered customers who
were already emotionally invested in the

103
00:06:44,629 --> 00:06:45,629
brand.

104
00:06:46,920 --> 00:06:51,261
As complaints continued, Coca-Cola
faced another difficult choice.

105
00:06:51,893 --> 00:06:55,970
It could defend the reformulation and
hope customers eventually accepted it.

106
00:06:56,550 --> 00:07:00,754
Or it could acknowledge that replacing
the original had been a mistake.

107
00:07:01,287 --> 00:07:05,631
Reversing course meant publicly retreating
from one of the most heavily researched

108
00:07:05,671 --> 00:07:08,321
product decisions in
the company's history.

109
00:07:08,754 --> 00:07:13,296
But the company also had evidence that
its original formula retained powerful

110
00:07:13,336 --> 00:07:14,336
demand.

111
00:07:14,566 --> 00:07:17,578
Leadership ultimately
decided to bring it back.

112
00:07:18,060 --> 00:07:21,752
The second decision would become
almost as famous as the first.

113
00:07:22,312 --> 00:07:25,592
Coca-Cola was about to restore
the product it had removed.

114
00:07:27,430 --> 00:07:33,028
On July eleventh, 1985, Coca-Cola announced
that its original formula was returning

115
00:07:33,068 --> 00:07:35,598
under the name Coca-Cola Classic.

116
00:07:36,195 --> 00:07:40,870
Only seventy-nine days had passed since
the launch of the reformulated drink.

117
00:07:41,368 --> 00:07:44,209
The announcement became
major national news.

118
00:07:44,848 --> 00:07:46,156
Customers celebrated.

119
00:07:46,853 --> 00:07:51,652
The company's consumer hotline received
thirty-one thousand six hundred calls in the

120
00:07:51,692 --> 00:07:53,602
two days following the announcement.

121
00:07:54,282 --> 00:07:58,704
For Coca-Cola, the response demonstrated
that the original product possessed a kind

122
00:07:58,744 --> 00:08:02,254
of value the taste tests had
not adequately captured.

123
00:08:02,813 --> 00:08:04,795
The company had changed the formula.

124
00:08:05,379 --> 00:08:07,660
Its customers had forced it to reconsider.

125
00:08:09,514 --> 00:08:13,744
The return of Coca-Cola Classic did
not mean New Coke vanished overnight.

126
00:08:14,378 --> 00:08:19,184
For a period, the company sold both
products, each with distinct advertising.

127
00:08:19,750 --> 00:08:23,972
The original formula reclaimed its familiar
position, while the reformulated version

128
00:08:24,012 --> 00:08:25,702
continued as an alternative.

129
00:08:26,292 --> 00:08:30,704
New Coke was later renamed Coke II and
eventually disappeared from the American

130
00:08:30,744 --> 00:08:31,744
market.

131
00:08:31,970 --> 00:08:36,890
This detail matters because the reversal
was not simply the cancellation of a failed

132
00:08:36,930 --> 00:08:37,930
launch.

133
00:08:38,086 --> 00:08:42,791
Coca-Cola changed its product
architecture to restore customer choice.

134
00:08:43,389 --> 00:08:47,871
The brand could offer a new taste without
forcing loyal consumers to surrender the

135
00:08:47,911 --> 00:08:48,911
old one.

136
00:08:50,234 --> 00:08:54,705
The backlash and successful return of
Coca-Cola Classic later encouraged speculation

137
00:08:54,745 --> 00:08:58,775
that New Coke had been an
elaborate publicity stunt.

138
00:08:59,243 --> 00:09:01,980
But that explanation lacks
convincing evidence.

139
00:09:02,664 --> 00:09:07,372
Coca-Cola's own historical account describes
a genuine attempt to strengthen its

140
00:09:07,412 --> 00:09:08,922
competitive position.

141
00:09:09,466 --> 00:09:13,694
Contemporary reporting also documents
the extensive research and management

142
00:09:13,734 --> 00:09:15,584
confidence behind the decision.

143
00:09:16,156 --> 00:09:20,876
The return of the original formula
ultimately generated enormous attention and

144
00:09:20,916 --> 00:09:22,846
renewed affection for the brand.

145
00:09:23,411 --> 00:09:25,866
That doesn't mean the company
engineered the disaster.

146
00:09:26,532 --> 00:09:30,258
An unintended recovery is
not proof of a secret plan.

147
00:09:31,988 --> 00:09:35,801
The New Coke case exposes four
failures in decision-making.

148
00:09:36,370 --> 00:09:40,744
First, management focused heavily
on a measurable attribute: taste.

149
00:09:41,386 --> 00:09:45,903
Second, it underestimated emotional
attachment to the existing product.

150
00:09:46,409 --> 00:09:50,911
Third, it treated replacement as the
preferred strategy instead of preserving the

151
00:09:50,951 --> 00:09:53,021
original alongside an alternative.

152
00:09:53,684 --> 00:09:58,433
And fourth, it assumed strong research
results would translate into consumer

153
00:09:58,473 --> 00:09:59,473
acceptance.

154
00:09:59,891 --> 00:10:04,040
Yet the case also demonstrates the
value of correcting a decision quickly.

155
00:10:04,653 --> 00:10:08,815
Coca-Cola listened to the backlash
and restored what customers wanted.

156
00:10:09,264 --> 00:10:14,367
Its recovery depended on recognizing that
the original product represented more than

157
00:10:14,407 --> 00:10:15,407
its ingredients.

158
00:10:17,128 --> 00:10:19,812
Coca-Cola didn't fail
because it ignored research.

159
00:10:20,390 --> 00:10:25,109
It failed because it trusted one kind of
research to answer a much larger question.

160
00:10:25,644 --> 00:10:30,191
The company knew which drink many
consumers preferred in a blind taste test.

161
00:10:30,721 --> 00:10:35,614
It did not fully understand what replacing
Coca-Cola would mean to the people who

162
00:10:35,654 --> 00:10:36,654
loved it.

163
00:10:36,997 --> 00:10:39,802
Seventy-nine days later,
the original returned.

164
00:10:40,415 --> 00:10:42,978
The lesson extends far beyond soft drinks.

165
00:10:43,561 --> 00:10:47,199
Customers don't always value a
product only for what it does.

166
00:10:47,766 --> 00:10:50,170
Sometimes they value what it represents.

167
00:10:50,930 --> 00:10:55,727
And changing that without understanding
the consequences can turn innovation into

168
00:10:55,767 --> 00:10:56,767
disaster.

169
00:10:57,067 --> 00:10:58,768
Every brand leaves evidence.

